//
// The wave LIST, and the schedule that walks it — what makes a base
// seven waves long instead of one.
//
// Plan 16 phase W1.  `docs/DESIGN.md` § Wave list authors a flat
// sequence of enemy counts with a fixed lull between them, and
// `examples/waves.json` holds it: `[5, 8, 12, 20, 30, 50, 80]`, fifteen
// seconds apart.  Until this file, nothing read either — a `.keys`
// script said `wave 8` and that was the whole of dryopea's pacing.
//
// ── ⚠ The schedule advances on a CLEAR, and needs no timeout ────
//
// `numbers.json` § wave_system.inter_wave_delay is *"wall-clock seconds
// between the last enemy dying and the next wave's spawn"*, so the next
// wave waits for the previous one to be dead.  Read quickly that sounds
// fragile — what if a wave never dies?
//
// **Measured (plan 16 W0): a base that cannot clear its wave is a run
// that has already ended.**  An undefended band playing a wave of five
// never cleared it at all; the wallet emptied at tick 74 with all five
// robots standing on the core.  A wall and one tower cleared the same
// wave at tick 165 and never fell.  So "the wave never clears" and "the
// player lost" are the same state, and a fallback timer would only
// start wave 2 over a base that is already over.
//
// ⚠ The one state W0 did NOT produce is a stalemate — a wave that can
// neither break in nor be killed.  `plans/16` § Open questions 1 keeps
// it open deliberately: the schedule stalls honestly rather than
// guessing, and a real map is what would settle it.
//
// ── ⚠ Neither timer needs an epsilon, and that is a DECISION ────
//
// Plan 15 C0 found that a banked timer loses a tick exactly when its
// duration divides the tick length exactly — 60 s of helper recovery is
// 90 ticks and a bare `> 0.0` gives 91.  Both timers here are the safe
// kind: 15.0 s is **22.5** ticks and 5.0 s is **7.5**, neither of them
// whole.
//
// So there is no epsilon in this file, and its absence is deliberate
// rather than an oversight — the note is here so that a reader
// comparing it with `helper.loft` does not add one, and so that anyone
// who rounds either number to a whole count of seconds knows they have
// just moved it into the dangerous class.
//
// ── ⚠ A wave is PARTS, and its size is DERIVED (plan 23 K1) ─────
//
// `plans/23` § Open questions 3 asked whether a wave row carries a mix
// or the list carries rows per class.  **It carries a mix**, as a flat
// `vector<WavePart>` keyed by wave index, and a wave's SIZE is summed
// from its parts rather than stored beside them.
//
// ⚠ **That deletes the plan's own negative control, and deleting it is
// the point.**  K1's gate named *"a mix whose parts do not sum to the
// wave's count is refused at parse"* — a check that only exists if the
// count is a second fact living beside the parts.  It is the shape
// `carry.loft` refuses in one line (*"a slot on the carrier and an
// owner on the object are two facts that can disagree"*), so the count
// is not stored, the sum cannot be violated, and there is nothing to
// refuse.  What replaces it as a control is in
// `tests/23_k1_composition.loft` § The size is the sum.
//
// ⚠ **`wave_schedule_of` keeps its signature and its meaning** — a
// `vector<integer>` is a list of all-REGULAR waves, which is what every
// scenario in the gate and every test written before K1 authors.  That
// is what lets composition land without moving a measurement.
//
// ⚠ **`WaveFile` is deliberately NOT extended.**  Nothing in `src/`
// loads it (only `tests/16_w1` does, against `examples/waves.json`), so
// giving it composition would be a file format nothing reads — and the
// shape it would need is `vector<Struct>` in a `text as`, which is the
// one that hangs the JSON cast and miscompiles to zero entries on the
// native backend (`CLAUDE.md` § Loft language gotchas).  Composition is
// authored in the `.keys` vocabulary, which is where dryopea authors
// everything else about a run.

use lattice;
use passable;
use tick_clock;
use tick_timer;

// ─── The authored list ──────────────────────────────────────────

// numbers.json § wave_system.inter_wave_delay, and the same figure in
// `waves.json`'s `inter_wave_delay_seconds`.
//
// ⚠ The fallback, for a run with no file loaded — `wave_list_default`
// is what a test or a scripted scenario gets.  The FILE is the source
// of truth when one is read.
pub const INTER_WAVE_DELAY_SECONDS: float = 15.0;

// One `waves.json`.
//
// ⚠ **This one really is LOADED**, which makes it the second file in
// dryopea after the palette and the exception to `CLAUDE.md`'s rule
// that every tunable is hand-copied into a `.loft` constant.  A `text
// as` over one scalar and a `vector<integer>` reads correctly
// (measured, plan 16 W0) — it is the `vector<Struct>` shape that hangs
// and that miscompiles to zero entries on the native backend.
pub struct WaveFile {
    inter_wave_delay_seconds: integer,
    waves: vector<integer>,
}

// ⚠ **Reading and parsing are SPLIT, and it is a loft bug rather than
// a taste** ([loft#908](https://github.com/loft-lang/loft/issues/908),
// `loft_repros/missing_file_struct_return.loft`).  A function that both
// reads a MISSING file's content and returns a STRUCT double-frees and
// SIGABRTs the interpreter — `BUG (#306): refused to free the stack
// store … a stack-record ref was treated as an owned heap store`.
//
// ⚠ **It is interpreter-only and the native backend is fine**, which is
// the worst possible direction: both of dryopea's gates run interpreted
// (`CLAUDE.md` § Both gates run INTERPRETED), so this is a crash the
// suite CAN see and a shipped build would not.
//
// ⚠ **The if-else expression form does NOT dodge it** — the workaround
// `CLAUDE.md` § Loft language gotchas records for `load_map_or_empty`'s
// early-return type error is a different bug and does not apply.  What
// works is putting the file read in a function of its own, so no single
// function both reads and returns a struct.

// The authored wave list as TEXT, or "" when the path cannot be read.
pub fn wave_file_text(path: text) -> text {
    wft_c = file(path).content() ?? "";
    wft_c
}

// Parse an authored wave list.  Answers an EMPTY file for empty text,
// so a caller can tell "no list" from "a list of one".
pub fn wave_file_parse(source: text) -> WaveFile {
    if source == "" {
        return WaveFile { inter_wave_delay_seconds: 0, waves: [] };
    }
    wfp_f = source as WaveFile;
    wfp_f
}

// Read the authored wave list.
pub fn wave_file_load(path: text) -> WaveFile {
    wfl_txt = wave_file_text(path);
    wfl_f   = wave_file_parse(wfl_txt);
    wfl_f
}

// The design's default list, for a run that loads no file.
pub fn wave_list_default() -> vector<integer> {
    [5, 8, 12, 20, 30, 50, 80]
}

// ─── What a wave is MADE OF (plan 23 K1) ────────────────────────

// One class's share of one wave.
//
// ⚠ **`wave` is the index into the list, and the vector is FLAT** —
// there is no vector-of-vectors, so wave 2 is "every part whose `wave`
// is 2, in the order they sit in the vector".  Order is authored order
// and it is load-bearing: plan 23 K0 measured that the same mix chews a
// wall 20x faster with the miners behind the scouts than in front of
// them, because the breach clock saturates past a handful of attackers.
//
// ⚠ **`count` is the only place a number of robots is written down.**
// Nothing stores a wave's total; `wave_schedule_size` sums it.
pub struct WavePart {
    wave:  integer,
    count: integer,
    kind:  integer,     // `passable.loft` § ENEMY_KIND_*
}

// ─── The schedule ───────────────────────────────────────────────

// Where a run is in its wave list.
//
// ⚠ **`sent` counts waves ALREADY SPAWNED**, not the one to come, so
// zero is the neutral value — a `WaveSchedule { }` literal is a run
// that has sent nothing, which is what a fresh run is.  It is the same
// rule `damage.loft` keeps with *damage taken* and `wallet.loft` with
// *points spent*, and here it also makes `sent` the index of the next
// wave with no arithmetic.
//
// ⚠ `running` is separate from `sent > 0` because a schedule that has
// not STARTED is not the same as one that has finished — plan 16 W3
// puts a trigger in front of this, and until it fires a run has a list
// and is not playing it.
//
// ⚠ **`length` is how many waves the list HOLDS and cannot be derived**
// from the parts: a wave of zero robots is a slot in the list that
// passes, and `max(part.wave) + 1` cannot see a trailing one.  It is a
// LENGTH rather than a second copy of the composition — the invariant
// every factory here keeps is *every index in `0..length` carries at
// least one part*, which is what makes `wave_schedule_size` total.
pub struct WaveSchedule {
    parts:   vector<WavePart>,
    length:  integer,     // how many waves the list holds
    sent:    integer,     // waves spawned so far; also the next index
    lull:    Timer,       // the wait before the next wave
    delay:   integer,     // base time units between waves
    running: boolean,
}

// One all-REGULAR part per entry — what a `vector<integer>` list means.
fn regular_parts(counts: vector<integer>) -> vector<WavePart> {
    rp_out: vector<WavePart> = [];
    rp_i = 0;
    for rp_c in counts {
        rp_out += [WavePart { wave: rp_i, count: rp_c,
                              kind: ENEMY_KIND_REGULAR }];
        rp_i += 1;
    }
    rp_out
}

// Factory: a run playing an explicit list — what a scenario uses when
// it wants two short waves rather than the design's seven.
//
// ⚠ **A `vector<integer>` still means what it always meant**: N waves
// of N counts, every robot a regular.  Composition is layered on top by
// `wave_schedule_compose`, which is why plan 23 K1 moved no existing
// reading — a list authored before composition existed still describes
// the same run.
pub fn wave_schedule_of(waves: vector<integer>, delay: float) -> WaveSchedule {
    wso_p = regular_parts(waves);
    WaveSchedule { parts: wso_p, length: len(waves), sent: 0,
                   lull: timer_new(),
                   delay: clock_units_from_seconds(delay), running: false }
}

// Factory: a run with the design's list, not yet started.
//
// ⚠ The call is BOUND rather than returned in tail position: a struct
// returned through two nested tail calls loses what its loop wrote
// ([loft#880](https://github.com/loft-lang/loft/issues/880)), and
// `wave_schedule_of` builds its parts in a loop.
pub fn wave_schedule_new() -> WaveSchedule {
    wsn_s = wave_schedule_of(wave_list_default(), INTER_WAVE_DELAY_SECONDS);
    wsn_s
}

// Start the schedule.  The first wave goes out on the next tick.
//
// ⚠ **Wave 1 does not wait for a lull**, because the lull is *between*
// waves: `DESIGN.md` § Wave-1 triggers says wave 1 fires when its
// trigger fires, and a fifteen-second pause after the player provoked
// something would read as the trigger not having worked.
pub fn wave_schedule_begin(ws: WaveSchedule) {
    ws.running = true;
    timer_cancel(ws.lull);
}

pub fn wave_schedule_running(ws: const WaveSchedule) -> boolean {
    ws.running
}

// How many waves this run has sent.
pub fn wave_schedule_sent(ws: const WaveSchedule) -> integer {
    ws.sent
}

// How many waves the list holds.
pub fn wave_schedule_count(ws: const WaveSchedule) -> integer {
    ws.length
}

// What wave `i` is made of, in the order it will be spawned.  An empty
// vector for a wave the list does not have.
pub fn wave_schedule_parts(ws: const WaveSchedule, i: integer) -> vector<WavePart> {
    wsp_out: vector<WavePart> = [];
    for wsp_p in ws.parts {
        if wsp_p.wave == i { wsp_out += [wsp_p]; }
    }
    wsp_out
}

// Is wave `i` the plain kind — one group, and it is regulars?
//
// ⚠ **The question is about the SHAPE and not about the outcome.**  A
// wave of `4 robot 8 robot` is two groups that spawn exactly as one of
// twelve would, and it still answers false — because `emit.loft` uses
// this to decide whether a `compose` line is needed, and a capture that
// wrote one group where the state holds two replays into a different
// state.  `tests/18_s2` compares the STATE, so a merge here would fail
// there rather than here.
pub fn wave_schedule_pure(ws: const WaveSchedule, i: integer) -> boolean {
    wsr_n    = 0;
    wsr_only = true;
    for wsr_p in ws.parts {
        if wsr_p.wave == i {
            wsr_n += 1;
            if wsr_p.kind != ENEMY_KIND_REGULAR { wsr_only = false; }
        }
    }
    wsr_n == 1 && wsr_only
}

// Replace what wave `i` is made of.  Answers false — and changes
// nothing — when `i` names no wave, or when the composition is empty.
//
// ⚠ **It REPLACES rather than adds**, which is what makes the `.keys`
// verb a statement (*wave 2 is four miners and eight scouts*) rather
// than an accumulation whose result depends on how many times the line
// was played.  Re-running a fixture has to reach the same state.
//
// ⚠ **The wave index is STAMPED here** — a caller passes the groups and
// this writes `wave: i` onto each, so a part cannot end up filed under
// a wave that is not the one being composed.
//
// ⚠ **An empty composition is refused**, and that is what keeps *every
// index carries at least one part* true.  A wave of nothing is
// `compose {i} 0 robot`, which says so.
pub fn wave_schedule_compose(ws: WaveSchedule, i: integer,
                             parts: vector<WavePart>) -> boolean {
    if i < 0 || i >= ws.length { return false; }
    if len(parts) == 0 { return false; }

    // Rebuilt in wave order, so the vector stays grouped and a capture
    // reads down the list rather than round it.
    wsc_out: vector<WavePart> = [];
    wsc_w = 0;
    while wsc_w < ws.length {
        if wsc_w == i {
            for wsc_n in parts {
                wsc_out += [WavePart { wave: i, count: wsc_n.count,
                                       kind: wsc_n.kind }];
            }
        } else {
            for wsc_p in ws.parts {
                if wsc_p.wave == wsc_w { wsc_out += [wsc_p]; }
            }
        }
        wsc_w += 1;
    }
    ws.parts = wsc_out;
    true
}

// Has every wave been sent?
//
// ⚠ **Sent, not cleared** — `DESIGN.md` § Wave 7 cleared → free
// scramble needs both, and the second one is `alive_count` at the call
// site.  One question per function, because "the list is finished" and
// "the battlefield is empty" become different at exactly the moment
// that section is about.
pub fn wave_schedule_done(ws: const WaveSchedule) -> boolean {
    ws.sent >= ws.length
}

// The size of wave `i`, or 0 when the list has no such wave.
//
// ⚠ **SUMMED, never stored** — this is the whole of plan 23 K1's data
// decision.  There is no field a mix could disagree with, so *the parts
// sum to the count* is not an invariant anybody has to check.
pub fn wave_schedule_size(ws: const WaveSchedule, i: integer) -> integer {
    wss_n = 0;
    for wss_p in ws.parts {
        if wss_p.wave == i { wss_n += wss_p.count; }
    }
    wss_n
}

// Base time units left before the next wave.
//
// ⚠ **Integer since plan 26 L3.**  It used to be float seconds, and the
// lull was one of the two timers in the game that never got an epsilon:
// counted down through floats it ran a tick LONG at three of seven tick
// lengths (`@M033`).
pub fn wave_schedule_lull(ws: const WaveSchedule) -> integer {
    timer_left(ws.lull)
}

// Base time units between waves, as this schedule was authored.
pub fn wave_schedule_delay(ws: const WaveSchedule) -> integer {
    ws.delay
}

// Spend one tick of the lull, and answer the size of the wave to send
// NOW — or 0 for "not yet".
//
// ⚠ **It decides and does not spawn**, because spawning needs the
// marker world and the core, and a schedule that knew about those could
// not be tested without building a map.  `spawn.loft::wave_schedule_
// tick` is the half that puts enemies on the ground.
//
// ⚠ `cleared` is passed IN rather than read, for the same reason: the
// roster belongs to the wave engine.  It is `alive_count(state) == 0`
// at the only call site.
pub fn wave_schedule_due(ws: WaveSchedule, cleared: boolean,
                         elapsed: integer) -> integer {
    if !ws.running { return 0; }
    if wave_schedule_done(ws) { return 0; }

    // A wave is in the world: nothing to do, and the lull has not
    // started.  ⚠ The `sent > 0` guard is what stops the FIRST wave
    // being treated as a cleared one — a fresh run has an empty
    // battlefield, which is `cleared` in every sense except the one
    // that matters.
    if ws.sent > 0 {
        if !cleared { return 0; }
        timer_spend(ws.lull, elapsed);
        if timer_running(ws.lull) { return 0; }
    }

    // ⚠ The SIZE, not the composition: this half of the split knows
    // only a clock, and `spawn.loft::wave_schedule_step` asks
    // `wave_schedule_parts` for what to put on the ground.  A wave of
    // zero still consumes its slot and still answers 0, which reads as
    // "not yet" to the caller and spawns nothing either way.
    wsd_size = wave_schedule_size(ws, ws.sent);
    ws.sent  = ws.sent + 1;
    timer_arm(ws.lull, ws.delay);
    wsd_size
}
